integrated_rules:
If (in the relevant contexts) AI increases convenience in ways that improve people’s health and standard of living, makes work/study easier for students and professionals, improves accessibility for people with disabilities, and can improve workplace safety by monitoring conditions/flagging risks, then: 
- conclude “AI is good for society” if the available evidence does not show that AI use causes net harms that outweigh these benefits; 
- conclude “AI is not good for society” if the available evidence shows scams and decision errors harm the economic well-being of many people and businesses to the extent that these harms outweigh the above benefits; 
- otherwise (i.e., evidence is insufficient to establish whether harms outweigh benefits), the conclusion is unresolved.
